1-1 enemies: Goomba x2, Green Koopa Troopa x1 (near the middle), Goomba, ... \n\nHonestly, easier to find empirically by walking/teleporting.\n\nNew robust plan: Instead of teleporting camera, I'll teleport Mario to a safe open location just ahead of the current camera and use the engine's natural scroll. To find a safe location, I can teleport Mario to the same absolute position as the camera + 100 and hold right; if stuck, try a different y (put him in the air) — but he'll fall.\n\nActually, the reason Mario was stuck at abs 1000: maybe there's a pipe there. Place Mario in the air above and let him land: set $00CE (y) = 100 and $00B5 = 1, and set x. Then hold right; he'll fall onto whatever ground is there and walk.\n\nBut the deeper issue: does $073F follow the camera variable? Let me verify by a clean test: write camera=600 again and read $073F after stepping.\n\nActually, let's step back and check whether the camera variable is really $071A/$071C. When I wrote $071C=232 and Mario=232..., the engine kept them (didn't overwrite). And $073F stayed 196. So $071C is not the scroll source used for rendering; the scroll source is $073F. The engine must write $073F from its internal camera. Where's the internal camera? Possibly $071A/$071B! Let's reconsider: maybe camera 16-bit is $071A (low) and $071B (high)? We read $071A=0,$071B=1 at start → 0x0100 = 256? but initial scroll was 153 at start (well, camera was 153 when I first looked, and $071A=0,$071B=1). Hmm 256 ≠ 153.\n\nWait, at that time Mario abs = 347, screen x=194, so camera=153. And $071C=153. So $071C matched camera exactly. And $073F=153. So both $071C and $073F were 153.\n\nSo normally $073F=$071C=camera. When I wrote $071C, the engine should copy to $073F... but it didn't because Mario is stuck/motionless so the scroll routine didn't run.\n\nThe scroll routine likely runs every frame in the game engine ($AEDC mode-1 handler), setting $073F from the camera ($071A/$071C). But $073F stayed 196 while camera 1