sassembly \"EnemyData\"):\nI recall 1-1 enemy data is:\n```\n1E 00 00  (Goomba)\n...\n```\nHmm. Actually from the SMB disassembly, the 1-1 enemies:\n```\nAreaDataOffset...\nEnemyData:\n  .db $1E, $C2, $00   ; Goomba\n  .db $6B, $06, $8B   ; ?\n  ...\n```\nHmm, so byte1=0x1E for the first Goomba, not 0x30. Wait our first byte is 0x30! 0x30 = 48. Hmm. Maybe the enemy data starts at 0x1F01 but our first byte read was 30. Actually maybe the list starts with a byte that's part of a page marker? Hmm.\n\nThe known SMB 1-1 enemy data (from the disassembly, with comments):\n```\n    .db $1E, $C2, $00    ; Goomba\n    .db $6B, $06, $8B    ; Goomba? \n```\nNo.\n\nLet me just empirically find the Koopa by continuing. Actually — better: let me check the enemy type table. In the SMB disassembly, the \"EnemyObjectType\" values used in level data are:\n$00 Goomba\n$01 GreenKoopa... no.\n\nActually level data byte3 uses a different encoding: The enemy type is the full byte, and the type is looked up in a table of pointers indexed by... hmm. In the SMB disassembly, the spawner (EnemyObjects) does:\nLDA EnemyData,x -> stores to Enemy_X\n... and the type byte is stored in $0016. Then a routine \"InitEnemyObject\" uses the type byte.\n\nGiven the type bytes we saw (00, 8B, B7, 35, 5B, 37, 0F, 37), and the fact that the game only has a handful of enemy types, these must be indices into a table of ~64 entries where similar enemies have different speeds/directions. Indeed SMB's enemy table has many entries. Green Koopa Troopa (walking left) might be 0x35 or 0x37!\n\nHmm. Actually in SMB, the enemy type IDs (from \"EnemyObjects\" table):\n```\nEnemyObjectTypes:\n$00 Goomba\n$01 Green Koopa\n$02 Buzzy Beetle\n$03 Red Koopa\n$04 Piranha Plant?\n...\n```\nBut the level data byte for Goomba is often $00. And a Koopa is $01? Not matching 8B/B7.\n\nHmm, maybe the level data enemy byte3 includes flags in upper bits. E.g. bit7 = \"doesn't move\"? Let me look:\n8B = 1000 1011\nB7 = 1011 0111\n35 = 0011 0101\n5B = 0101 1011\n37 = 0011 0111\n0F = 0000 1111\n\nIf low 5-6 bits are the type: 8B & 0x3F = 0x0B (11); B7 & 0x3F = 0x37 (55); 35 & 0x3F=0x35 (53); 5B&0x3F=0x1B(27); 37&0x3F=0x37(55); 0F&0x3F=0x0F(15). Still large.\n\nHmm, maybe the type is the whole byte minus some base. Whatever.\n\nLet me take the empirical approach: continue to the end, then go back and systematically scan for the Koopa. But scanning takes many cycles.\n\nAlternative: I can read the live enemy slots while walking. Let me read $000F-$0014 (active), $0016-$001B (type), $006E-$0073 (page), $0087-$008C (x low), $00CF-$00D4 (y) right now. There are two Goombas active. This will let me correlate the type byte with Goomba. Then when I see a different type/position, I can identify the Koopa.\n\nLet me read those arrays now. Addresses: 15 (0x0F) len 6; 22 (0x16) len 6; 110 (0x6E) len 6; 135 (0x87) len 6; 207 (0xCF) len 6.\n\nActually the first Goomba is at screen x141 → abs = camera+141. Camera = 1897-239 = 1658... wait Mario screen x 239. Camera = 1897-239 = 1658. Goomba abs = 1658+141 = 1799 (page 7 low 7).\n\nLet me read."}
