{"type":"thinking","text":"The pole is in nametable 0 at tile_x=31, tile_y=5 (ball) and tile_y=12 (shaft). So the nametable column 31 is the pole. Let me read nametable 0 column 30-31 for rows 4-25. I already did: col31 all $24 except row5 (ball $2F) and rows 6-25... wait the earlier read showed col31 all $24 for rows 4-25! But get_tile_at_pixel says col31 row5 = $2F and row12 = $A2. Contradiction.\n\nThe earlier read_nametable(30,4,2,22) gave:\nRow 4: 24 24\nRow 5: 24 24  ← but tile_x31 row5 should be $2F\nRow 6: 24 24\nRow 7: 38 24\n...\n\nHmm, so read_nametable read the WRONG nametable (nametable 0 base $2000) while get_tile_at_pixel reads $2000+0x18B*... Actually nametable_addr 8364 = 0x20AC → nametable 0. And read_nametable(0, x=30,y=4) reads $2000 + 4*32 + 30 = $2000+158 = 0x209E. Row5 col31 = 0x20BF. get_tile says nametable_addr 8364=0x20AC = row5*32+12 = 160+12 → col 12?! \n\nWait 0x20AC - 0x2000 = 0xAC = 172. 172/32 = 5.375 → row 5, col 12. So the ball tile is at nametable col 12, row 5, NOT col 31!\n\nBut get_tile_at_pixel reported tile_x=31. Hmm, tile_x is the screen tile column (248/8=31), while nametable_addr is the actual nametable address. So the nametable column is 12 (0xAC = row5 col12). And nametable 0.\n\nSo the pole is at nametable 0, col 12. The scroll makes screen x 248 = (scroll 104 + 248) = 352 → mod 256 = 96 → /8 = 12. Yes! col 12. Good.\n\nSo I should read_nametable(0, x=12, y=4, width=2, height=22). Let me do that to find the flag tiles.\n\nActually the flag hangs to the left of the pole, so it might be at col 11 (a wider flag). Let me read cols 9-13, rows 3-25.\n\nLet me do read_nametable(0, x=9, y=3, width=5, height=23).\n\nHmm, but by now the flag has slid down. Let me first check if the flag is still up or down. Actually the flag slides down with Mario, so it's near Mario (y~150). In nametable terms, col 12, row ~18.\n\nLet me read the nametable columns 9-13 rows 4-25 and look for non-blank tiles."}
