tive: The flag is a background object that gets moved by writing tiles to the nametable. When Mario grabs the pole, the game copies the flag tile to VRAM at the flag's new position. Since the flag hangs to the LEFT of the pole (cols 10-11), and those are blank now, the flag was erased as it moved.\n\nHmm, but the flag should still be at the bottom now (before Mario reaches the bottom, the flag waits at the pole base). Actually in SMB, when Mario slides down, the flag slides down ahead of him and stops at the bottom; when Mario reaches the bottom, the flag then... stays? And Mario walks to the castle. So the flag should be visible at the pole base.\n\nLooking at the nametable rows 24-25 col12/13 = $AB-$AE. Those might BE the flag! Not a base. Hmm, in SMB1 the flagpole base is a green block that's actually the \"pole base\" (looks like a small green block). And the flag at the bottom would be draped at the base.\n\nHmm, rows 24-25 at col12-13: tiles $AB,$AD,$AC,$AE. If those are the flag, then the flag is 2x2 tiles at the bottom. But the flag is white and would render via palette 1? They're palette 1. Palette 1 in 1-1 is [$0F,$36,$17,$0F] = black, peach, brown, black. Peach/brown — that's the flag colors? The SMB1 flag is white? Hmm, no — the flag might be peach/white. Actually the flag in SMB is white with a green? Or is it the \"flag\" is actually reddish?\n\nHold on. Let me just look at the current screenshot: at the pole location (right edge, x~248-255, y~24 and y~150-160), what's there? The screenshot shows at top-right (x~250,y~25) a small green blob = the ball. At y~150-160, x~250, there's a brown/peach object = could be the flag at the base!\n\nLet me sample pixels there. Actually earlier at (250,160) I got red $16 (Mario). Now Mario is elsewhere. Let me sample (250,155),(248,155),(252,155),(250,150).\n\nLet me do that."}
