e Mario?\n- Palette 2: green/white (Koopa, mushroom 1UP)\n- Palette 3: ...\n\nHmm. Actually, in SMB the sprite palettes are:\nPalette 4: Mario\nPalette 5: Fire Mario\nPalette 6: [0F,16,27,30]? \nPalette 7: ...\n\nHmm. The Koopa Troopa (green) uses palette 6? Actually I recall Koopa Troopa uses palette 6 maybe.\n\nHmm, the enemy I just made uses palette 6 (per OAM attr 2) with tiles $A5-$A8. Given the palette (red $16, white $30, orange $27), it would be red/white/orange, not green. So that's probably a \"Buzzy Beetle\"? Buzzy Beetle is dark. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the tiles. read_tile 0xA5, 0xA6, 0xA7, 0xA8.\n\nActually, more useful: let me cycle through type values and read the tiles for each, then compare to known Koopa graphics. But I want the green Koopa specifically.\n\nHmm, hold on. Big realization: the \"Goomba\" in 1-1 might actually use palette 7 = [0F,0F,36,17] = black/peach/brown. But a green Koopa would need a green palette. In 1-1, all 4 sprite palettes are fixed. Let me check get_all_palettes now to see palettes 4-7.\n\nActually, in SMB, sprite palettes are per-area and in the overworld: palette 4 = Mario, palette 5 = fire Mario/some items, palette 6 = ?, palette 7 = ?. The Koopa is green. So one of palettes 5,6,7 must contain green. Palette 5 in 1-1 (from fire_flower mapping) = [0F,1A,30,27] = black, green, white, orange. The Koopa Troopa is green+white — could use palette 5? Hmm, but the fire flower uses palette 5. Since the fire flower isn't in 1-1 normally (only from a block), palette 5 might be shared.\n\nActually in SMB, the Koopa Troopa uses the palette that has green. Let me just try setting the type byte to various values and see which gives a green turtle.\n\nLet me try type 2, 3, 4, 5 quickly. Each: write 22=v, step 1, read_tile of the resulting tiles (or screenshot). Screenshots are more informative for color.\n\nLet me screenshot the current type 1 first.\n\nActually, let me be efficient: I'll try type 3 and screenshot, type 4, etc. But list_sprites gives tiles, and I can read_tile to see the shape (not color). Color comes from palette. Let me use screenshot.\n\nLet me screenshot now (type 1)."}
